  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how two different organic cations affect the stability of a perovskite-like crystal under heat and light. In both cases, the starting material is drawn as a layered lattice of corner-sharing octahedra with small ions inside and larger organic molecules sitting above the surface. The central arrow indicates the same stress condition for both examples: heating at 85 °C under one-sun illumination.\n\nThe key message is that the top system degrades, while the bottom one remains more stable. In panel a, the surface-bound ammonium cation appears to lose a proton under stress. The schematic shows deprotonation producing ammonia and leaving a neutral species, and the lattice on the right looks disrupted, with the note that deprotonation leads to degradation. This suggests that proton loss destabilizes the material.\n\nIn panel b, a modified cation containing an additional iminium/guanidinium-like unit appears to retain its positive charge during the same treatment. The crystal framework on the right remains largely intact, and the text indicates that cations with higher pKa improve thermal stability. Overall, the figure illustrates a design principle: using less easily deprotonated cations helps preserve the perovskite structure under operating conditions.",
